<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-triple-integrals-cylindrical" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Triple Integrals in Cylindrical Coordinates</title>

<p>
Previously, we learned how to calculate triple integrals in rectangular
coordinates. Today, we will learn triple integrals in cylindrical
coordinates, which can be used to evaluate volumes of solids or to
integrate a function over a region in space. We will see that when
there is symmetry about an axis, it is more convenient to use
cylindrical coordinates to calculate a triple integral.
</p>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-cylindrical-coordinates">
<title>Cylindrical Coordinates</title>

<p>
Cylindrical coordinates <m>(r,\theta,z)</m> are the combination of
polar coordinates <m>(r,\theta)</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane and the
<m>z</m>-coordinate of Cartesian coordinates; see
<xref ref="fig-cylindrical-point-tikz"/>. Thus, adding <m>z</m> to
<xref ref="eq-polar-cartesian"/>, the relations between
Cartesian and cylindrical coordinates are
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-cyl-to-rect">
x = r\cos\theta, \qquad y = r\sin\theta, \qquad z = z,
</men>

<me>
r^2 = x^2 + y^2, \qquad \tan\theta = \frac{y}{x}, \qquad z = z.
</me>

<figure xml:id="fig-cylindrical-point-tikz">
<caption>
A point <m>P(r,\theta,z)</m> in cylindrical coordinates:
<m>(r,\theta)</m> are the polar coordinates of its projection
<m>(r,\theta,0)</m> into the <m>xy</m>-plane, and <m>z</m> is its
usual height.
</caption>
<image width="80%">
<shortdescription>
A point in space located by a radius in the plane, an angle, and a height.
</shortdescription>
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space. A point P is shown above the x y
plane. A segment of length r runs from the origin to the projection of
P in the x y plane, making an angle theta with the positive x axis,
and a vertical segment of length z connects the projection to P.
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// A point P(r, theta, z) in cylindrical coordinates.
// P = (1.3, 1.35, 1.55): its projection (r, theta, 0) is (1.3, 1.35, 0).
import graph3;

size(11cm);
// Same viewpoint as the pgfplots figure (view={125}{20}).
currentprojection = orthographic((0.7698, 0.539, 0.342));
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);
pen oipurple     = rgb(204/255, 121/255, 167/255);

triple P = (1.3, 1.35, 1.55);
triple Pxy = (P.x, P.y, 0);
real theta0 = atan2(P.y, P.x);

// axes
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(16pt)), O--2.1X, axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(16pt)), O--2.3Y, axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(16pt)), O--2.1Z, axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

// the angle theta in the xy-plane, lightly shaded
triple thetapt(pair t) { return t.y*(cos(t.x), sin(t.x), 0); }
draw(surface(thetapt, (0, 0), (theta0, 0.5), 8, 1, Spline),
     0.3*oipurple + 0.7*white + opacity(0.5), light=nolight);
draw(arc(O, 0.5X, 0.5*unit(Pxy)), oipurple + linewidth(1.0pt), Arrow3(size=6));

// projection segment r and vertical segment z
draw(O--Pxy, oivermillion + linewidth(1.4pt));
draw(Pxy--P, oiblue + linewidth(1.4pt));

// points
dot(P, oiblue + linewidth(5pt));
dot(Pxy, linewidth(4pt));

// labels
label("$P(r,\theta,z)$", P, 2N, oiblue + fontsize(14pt));
label("$(r,\theta,0)$", Pxy, 2S, fontsize(14pt));
label("$r$", 0.55*Pxy, 1.5SW, oivermillion + fontsize(14pt));
label("$z$", (P.x, P.y, P.z/2), 1.5E, oiblue + fontsize(14pt));
label("$\theta$", 0.75*(cos(theta0/2), sin(theta0/2), 0), oipurple + fontsize(14pt));
label("$O$", O, NW, fontsize(14pt));

// keep the z label inside the frame
dot((0, 0, 2.35), invisible);
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Before calculating triple integrals in cylindrical coordinates, we
need to figure out the volume element <m>dV</m> in this new set of
coordinates. Remember from <xref ref="eq-polar-area-element"/> that the
area element <m>dA</m> in polar
coordinates is <m>r\,dr\,d\theta</m>; stacking it through a height
<m>dz</m> gives the cylindrical volume element
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-dv-cylindrical">
dV = r\,dr\,d\theta\,dz.
</men>

<interactive geogebra="qjpb9re8"
             width="80%" aspect="60:49"
             material-width="600" material-height="490">
<instructions>
<p>
Use the three sliders to build the volume element one edge at a time.
The <m>dr</m> slider sets the radial thickness of the block, the
<m>d\theta</m> slider opens its angular width, and the <m>dz</m> slider
raises its height. Start with all three small, then increase them in
turn. The edges controlled by <m>dr</m> and <m>dz</m> are straight
segments of exactly those lengths, but the edge swept out by
<m>d\theta</m> is a circular arc of length <m>r\,d\theta</m><mdash/>it
grows in proportion to the block's distance <m>r</m> from the
<m>z</m>-axis. Slide <m>d\theta</m> alone and watch that curved edge
lengthen while the radial and vertical edges stay fixed.
</p>

<p>
Up close the block is essentially a rectangular box with edge lengths
<m>dr</m>, <m>r\,d\theta</m>, and <m>dz</m>, so multiplying the three
edges gives:
<me>
dV = dr \cdot r\,d\theta \cdot dz = r\,dr\,d\theta\,dz.
</me>
</p>

</instructions>
</interactive>

<p>
Now suppose our goal is to integrate over a region <m>E</m> described
as
</p>

<me>
E = \left\{(x,y,z)\ \middle|\ (x,y)\in D,\
u_1(x,y) \le z \le u_2(x,y)\right\},
</me>

<p>
where the region <m>D</m> is known in polar coordinates:
</p>

<me>
D = \left\{(r,\theta)\ \middle|\ \alpha\le\theta\le\beta,\
h_1(\theta) \le r \le h_2(\theta)\right\}.
</me>

<p>
Then the iterated integral <xref ref="eq-triple-iterated"/>, written as
<m>\iiint_E f\,dV
= \iint_D\big[\int_{u_1}^{u_2} f\,dz\big]dA</m>, can be converted to
cylindrical coordinates:
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-triple-cylindrical">
\iiint_E f(x,y,z)\,dV
= \int_{\alpha}^{\beta}\!\!\int_{h_1(\theta)}^{h_2(\theta)}
\!\!\int_{u_1(r\cos\theta,\,r\sin\theta)}^{u_2(r\cos\theta,\,r\sin\theta)}
f(r\cos\theta,\ r\sin\theta,\ z)\ r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta.
</men>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-cylindrical-mass-example">
<title>The Mass of a Solid With Axial Symmetry</title>

<example xml:id="example-cylindrical-mass">
<title>Example I</title>
<statement>
<p>
A solid <m>E</m> lies within the cylinder <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>, below
the plane <m>z = 4</m>, and above the paraboloid
<m>z = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m>. Given that the density of the solid at any
point is proportional to its distance from the axis of the cylinder,
find the mass of the solid.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
Let us begin by plotting the region of integration
(<xref ref="fig-cylindrical-solid-tikz"/>). Note that the equation of
the cylinder in cylindrical coordinates is <m>r = 1</m>, and the
equation of the paraboloid is
</p>

<me>
z = 1 - x^2 - y^2
= 1 - \left(r^2\cos^2\theta + r^2\sin^2\theta\right) = 1 - r^2.
</me>

<p>
Therefore, the limits of integration can be written as
</p>

<me>
E = \left\{(r,\theta,z)\ \middle|\ 0\le\theta\le 2\pi,\
0 \le r \le 1,\ 1 - r^2 \le z \le 4\right\},
</me>

<p>
since the projection into the <m>xy</m>-plane is a circle of radius
<m>1</m> and the height of the solid is bounded between the paraboloid
<m>z = 1 - r^2</m> and <m>z = 4</m>. Since we know that the density
<m>\delta(x,y,z)</m> of the solid is proportional to the distance from
the axis of the cylinder, we have
</p>

<me>
\delta(x,y,z) = K\sqrt{x^2 + y^2},
</me>

<p>
and since <m>r = \sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>, in cylindrical coordinates
<m>\delta(r,\theta,z) = Kr</m>. Thus, by <xref ref="eq-mass-triple"/>
and <xref ref="eq-triple-cylindrical"/>, we can compute the mass of the
solid as follows:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
m = \iiint_E K\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\,dV
\amp= \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^1\!\!\int_{1-r^2}^{4}
(Kr)\,r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^1
Kr^2\left[4 - \left(1 - r^2\right)\right]dr\,d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= K\int_0^{2\pi}\!d\theta\int_0^1\left(3r^2 + r^4\right)dr
</mrow>

<mrow>
\amp= 2\pi K\left[r^3 + \frac{r^5}{5}\right]_0^1
= \frac{12\pi K}{5}.
</mrow>
</md>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-cylindrical-solid-tikz">
<caption>
The solid of <xref ref="example-cylindrical-mass"/>: inside the
cylinder <m>r = 1</m>, above the paraboloid <m>z = 1 - r^2</m>, and
below the plane <m>z = 4</m>. Its mass, with density
<m>\delta = Kr</m>, is <m>\frac{12\pi K}{5}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
A vertical cylinder with a flat top and a dome-shaped paraboloid floor.
</shortdescription>
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space with a vertical circular cylinder of
radius one about the z axis, capped by the plane z equals four on top.
Inside the bottom of the cylinder, the paraboloid z equals one minus r
squared forms a dome shaped floor that touches the point zero zero one
on the z axis and meets the cylinder wall at the x y plane.
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// The solid inside the cylinder r = 1, above the paraboloid z = 1 - r^2
// and below the plane z = 4.
import graph3;

size(10.5cm);
// Same viewpoint as the pgfplots figure (view={125}{16}).
currentprojection = orthographic((0.7874, 0.5514, 0.2756));
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);

// paraboloid floor z = 1 - r^2 (param: r, theta, so that the normal points up)
triple floorpt(pair t) { return (t.x*cos(t.y), t.x*sin(t.y), 1 - t.x^2); }
// cylinder wall r = 1, z from 0 to 4 (param: theta, z; normal points out)
triple wallpt(pair t) { return (cos(t.x), sin(t.x), t.y); }
// top cap z = 4 (param: r, theta; normal points up)
triple cappt(pair t) { return (t.x*cos(t.y), t.x*sin(t.y), 4); }

draw(surface(floorpt, (0, 0), (1, 2pi), 4, 16, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.5*oivermillion + 0.5*white + opacity(0.75),
                         emissivepen=0.35*oivermillion, specularpen=gray(0.05)),
     meshpen=gray(0.3) + linewidth(0.3pt) + opacity(0.4));
draw(surface(wallpt, (0, 0), (2pi, 4), 16, 1, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.5*oiblue + 0.5*white + opacity(0.3),
                         emissivepen=0.3*oiblue, specularpen=gray(0.05)));
draw(surface(cappt, (0, 0), (1, 2pi), 1, 16, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.5*oiblue + 0.5*white + opacity(0.35),
                         emissivepen=0.3*oiblue, specularpen=gray(0.05)));

// rim of the top cap, and the base circle (dashed)
draw(circle((0, 0, 4), 1), oiblue + linewidth(1.2pt));
draw(circle(O, 1), oiblue + linewidth(1.0pt) + dashed);

// axes
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(20pt)), (-1.4, 0, 0)--(2.0, 0, 0), axispen,
     Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(20pt)), (0, -1.4, 0)--(0, 1.8, 0), axispen,
     Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(20pt)), O--(0, 0, 4.9), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

// the axis of the solid, dashed, from the floor to the top
draw((0, 0, 1)--(0, 0, 4), gray(0.35) + linewidth(0.7pt) + dashed);

// points and labels
dot((0, 0, 4), linewidth(4pt));
dot((0, 0, 1), linewidth(4pt));
dot((1, 0, 0), linewidth(4pt));
label("$(0,0,4)$", (0, 0, 4), 1.5E + 0.5N, fontsize(17pt));
label("$(0,0,1)$", (0, 0, 1), 1.5E + 0.5N, fontsize(17pt));
label("$(1,0,0)$", (1, 0, 0), 2S + 2E, fontsize(17pt));
// screen-left on the rim of the top cap is the direction (0.574, -0.819, 0)
label("$z = 4$", (0.574, -0.819, 4), 2W, fontsize(17pt));
label("$z = 1 - r^2$", (-0.574, 0.819, 0.6), 3E, oivermillion + fontsize(17pt));
label("$r = 1$", (-0.574, 0.819, 2.6), 3E, oiblue + fontsize(17pt));

// keep the z label inside the frame
dot((0, 0, 5.2), invisible);
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The way a column of the solid runs from the paraboloid floor up to the
plane <m>z = 4</m>, and how it sweeps around the axis, is animated in
<xref ref="fig-cylindrical-video"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cylindrical-video">
<caption>
Animation of the cylindrical setup for
<xref ref="example-cylindrical-mass"/>. The solid is assembled from
the cylinder <m>r = 1</m>, the plane <m>z = 4</m>, and the paraboloid
floor <m>z = 1 - r^2</m>; a column at <m>(r,\theta)</m> runs from
<m>z = 1-r^2</m> up to <m>z = 4</m>, and sweeps through the solid as
<m>\theta</m> goes around the axis, illustrating
<m>dV = r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-cylindrical-column"
       source="videos/cylindrical_column_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-cylindrical-questions">
<title>Concept Check</title>

<p>
Each part below describes a solid by the surfaces that bound it. Set up a
triple integral in cylindrical coordinates that gives its volume: the work
is all in the limits, and <xref ref="eq-dv-cylindrical"/> supplies the
rest. Try each part from the description alone<mdash/>the solution shows
the solid, so you can check whether the picture in your head was the right
one.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="q-cylindrical-volumes">
<title>Eight volumes in cylindrical coordinates</title>
<introduction>
<p>
Set up triple integrals, in cylindrical coordinates, which give the volume
of each of the following regions.
</p>
</introduction>
<task xml:id="task-cyl-a">
<statement>
<p>
The volume of the region bounded above by the paraboloid <m>z = 5-x^2-y^2</m>, below
by the plane <m>z=0</m>, and on the side by the cylinder
<m>x^2+y^2=4</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The bounding surfaces are the ones drawn in <xref ref="fig-cyl-a"/>, and
in cylindrical coordinates
</p>
<me>
V = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{2}\!\!\int_0^{\,5-r^2} r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta
  = 2\pi\int_0^2 \left(5r - r^3\right)dr = 12\pi .
</me>
<p>
The cylinder is <m>r=2</m> and the paraboloid is <m>z = 5-r^2</m>;
they meet at <m>z=1</m>, which is where the wall of the cylinder
stops.
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-cyl-a">
<caption>
The solid of part (a).
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="p4fvbdxf"
             width="100%" aspect="500:500" design-width="500"
             material-width="500" material-height="500"
             shift-drag-zoom="yes" reset-icon="yes">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the solid and compare it with the picture
you formed from the description. Shift-drag or pinch to zoom, and use
the reset icon in the corner to return to the original view.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
</solution>
</task>
<task xml:id="task-cyl-b">
<statement>
<p>
The volume of the region bounded below by the paraboloid
<m>z = \frac14\left(x^2+y^2\right)</m> and above by the plane
<m>z=1</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The bounding surfaces are the ones drawn in <xref ref="fig-cyl-b"/>, and
in cylindrical coordinates
</p>
<me>
V = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{2}\!\!\int_{r^2/4}^{1} r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta
  = 2\pi\int_0^2 \left(r - \frac{r^3}{4}\right)dr = 2\pi .
</me>
<p>
The paraboloid is <m>z = r^2/4</m>, and it meets <m>z=1</m> at
<m>r=2</m>, which fixes the outer limit of <m>r</m>.
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-cyl-b">
<caption>
The solid of part (b).
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="djjefngs"
             width="100%" aspect="500:500" design-width="500"
             material-width="500" material-height="500"
             shift-drag-zoom="yes" reset-icon="yes">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the solid and compare it with the picture
you formed from the description. Shift-drag or pinch to zoom, and use
the reset icon in the corner to return to the original view.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
</solution>
</task>
<task xml:id="task-cyl-c">
<statement>
<p>
The volume of the region bounded above by the paraboloid
<m>z = \frac14\left(x^2+y^2\right)</m>, below by the plane <m>z=0</m>,
and on the side by the cylinder <m>x^2+y^2=4</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The bounding surfaces are the ones drawn in <xref ref="fig-cyl-c"/>, and
in cylindrical coordinates
</p>
<me>
V = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{2}\!\!\int_0^{\,r^2/4} r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta
  = 2\pi\int_0^2 \frac{r^3}{4}\,dr = 2\pi .
</me>
<p>
This solid and the one of <xref ref="task-cyl-b"/> together fill the
cylinder <m>r \le 2</m>, <m>0 \le z \le 1</m>, whose volume is
<m>4\pi</m><mdash/>which is why both answers are <m>2\pi</m>.
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-cyl-c">
<caption>
The solid of part (c).
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="v4vbftuh"
             width="100%" aspect="500:500" design-width="500"
             material-width="500" material-height="500"
             shift-drag-zoom="yes" reset-icon="yes">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the solid and compare it with the picture
you formed from the description. Shift-drag or pinch to zoom, and use
the reset icon in the corner to return to the original view.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
</solution>
</task>
<task xml:id="task-cyl-d">
<statement>
<p>
The volume of the region bounded below by the lower half of the sphere
<m>x^2+y^2+(z-1)^2=4</m> and above by the plane <m>z=1</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The bounding surfaces are the ones drawn in <xref ref="fig-cyl-d"/>, and
in cylindrical coordinates
</p>
<me>
V = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{2}\!\!\int_{1-\sqrt{4-r^2}}^{1}
      r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta
  = 2\pi\int_0^2 r\sqrt{4-r^2}\,dr = \frac{16\pi}{3} .
</me>
<p>
The sphere has center <m>(0,0,1)</m> and radius <m>2</m>, so its lower
half is <m>z = 1-\sqrt{4-r^2}</m> and the answer is the volume
<m>\frac23\pi(2)^3</m> of a hemisphere of radius <m>2</m>.
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-cyl-d">
<caption>
The solid of part (d).
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="xunfqxxr"
             width="100%" aspect="500:500" design-width="500"
             material-width="500" material-height="500"
             shift-drag-zoom="yes" reset-icon="yes">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the solid and compare it with the picture
you formed from the description. Shift-drag or pinch to zoom, and use
the reset icon in the corner to return to the original view.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
</solution>
</task>
<task xml:id="task-cyl-e">
<statement>
<p>
The volume of the region bounded by the cone <m>z^2 = \frac14\left(x^2+y^2\right)</m>
and the planes <m>z=1</m> and <m>z=-1</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The bounding surfaces are the ones drawn in <xref ref="fig-cyl-e"/>, and
in cylindrical coordinates
</p>
<me>
V = 2\int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{2}\!\!\int_{r/2}^{1} r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta
  = 4\pi\int_0^2 \left(r - \frac{r^2}{2}\right)dr = \frac{8\pi}{3} .
</me>
<p>
The cone is <m>z = \pm r/2</m>, and a point lies inside its upper half
when <m>z \ge r/2</m>. The solid is two cones of radius <m>2</m> and
height <m>1</m>, each of volume <m>\frac13\pi(2)^2(1)</m>.
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-cyl-e">
<caption>
The solid of part (e).
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="p45dyezj"
             width="100%" aspect="500:500" design-width="500"
             material-width="500" material-height="500"
             shift-drag-zoom="yes" reset-icon="yes">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the solid and compare it with the picture
you formed from the description. Shift-drag or pinch to zoom, and use
the reset icon in the corner to return to the original view.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
</solution>
</task>
<task xml:id="task-cyl-f">
<statement>
<p>
The volume of the region bounded by the hyperboloid of one sheet
<m>x^2+y^2-z^2=4</m> and the planes <m>z=3</m> and <m>z=-3</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The bounding surfaces are the ones drawn in <xref ref="fig-cyl-f"/>, and
in cylindrical coordinates
</p>
<me>
V = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_{-3}^{3}\!\!\int_0^{\sqrt{z^2+4}}
      r\,dr\,dz\,d\theta
  = \pi\int_{-3}^{3}\left(z^2+4\right)dz = 42\pi .
</me>
<p>
Here it pays to integrate <m>r</m> first: the wall is
<m>r = \sqrt{z^2+4}</m>, one formula for every <m>z</m>, while slicing
the other way would take two pieces. The waist has radius <m>2</m> and
the caps radius <m>\sqrt{13}</m>.
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-cyl-f">
<caption>
The solid of part (f).
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="zpr8xcup"
             width="100%" aspect="500:500" design-width="500"
             material-width="500" material-height="500"
             shift-drag-zoom="yes" reset-icon="yes">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the solid and compare it with the picture
you formed from the description. Shift-drag or pinch to zoom, and use
the reset icon in the corner to return to the original view.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
</solution>
</task>
<task xml:id="task-cyl-g">
<statement>
<p>
The volume of the region bounded by the cone <m>z^2 = \frac14\left(x^2+y^2\right)</m>
and the planes <m>z=2</m> and <m>z=-1</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The bounding surfaces are the ones drawn in <xref ref="fig-cyl-g"/>, and
in cylindrical coordinates
</p>
<me>
V = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_{0}^{2}\!\!\int_0^{2z} r\,dr\,dz\,d\theta
    + \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_{-1}^{0}\!\!\int_0^{-2z} r\,dr\,dz\,d\theta
  = 12\pi .
</me>
<p>
The same cone as in <xref ref="task-cyl-e"/>, cut at different heights:
a piece of radius <m>4</m> and height <m>2</m> and one of radius
<m>2</m> and height <m>1</m>, of volumes <m>\frac{32\pi}{3}</m> and
<m>\frac{4\pi}{3}</m>.
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-cyl-g">
<caption>
The solid of part (g).
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="ckvwwrqq"
             width="100%" aspect="500:500" design-width="500"
             material-width="500" material-height="500"
             shift-drag-zoom="yes" reset-icon="yes">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the solid and compare it with the picture
you formed from the description. Shift-drag or pinch to zoom, and use
the reset icon in the corner to return to the original view.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
</solution>
</task>
<task xml:id="task-cyl-h">
<statement>
<p>
The volume of the region bounded above by the cone <m>z = 5-\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>, below
by the plane <m>z=0</m>, and on the side by the cylinder
<m>x^2+y^2=4</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The bounding surfaces are the ones drawn in <xref ref="fig-cyl-h"/>, and
in cylindrical coordinates
</p>
<me>
V = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{2}\!\!\int_0^{\,5-r} r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta
  = 2\pi\int_0^2 \left(5r - r^2\right)dr = \frac{44\pi}{3} .
</me>
<p>
The cone is <m>z = 5-r</m>, which is <m>3</m> at the wall <m>r=2</m>.
Compare <xref ref="task-cyl-a"/>: the same picture with <m>5-r</m> in
place of <m>5-r^2</m>.
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-cyl-h">
<caption>
The solid of part (h).
</caption>
